<div class="article-body">

<p>Run your grow light 24 hours a day and you might think you are being generous. I did. My microgreens grew slower, not faster. The tray on the left, under a light that turned off at night, outgrew the tray I left glowing around the clock.</p>

<p>Here is the short answer before anything else: <strong>most indoor plants want 12 to 16 hours of light a day, then 8 to 12 hours of total darkness.</strong> Seedlings lean toward 14 to 16. Houseplants are happy on the lower end. Nobody indoor should run a light 24/7.</p>

<p>Full disclosure: I am not a scientist. I am someone who killed a tray of microgreens being too eager. I have run lights indoors for 2 years, and the hours question was the last one I figured out. Let me save you the trial and error.</p>

<p>This is the part that surprised me: <strong>plants do not grow during the day. They grow at night.</strong> The light makes the sugar. The dark is when the plant actually builds cells with that sugar. Cut the dark, and you choke the growth engine. That single fact changed my whole setup.</p>

<h2 id="answer">1. The Short Answer: What to Set Today</h2>

<p>Use this as your starting point and adjust from there:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Seedlings and leafy greens:</strong> 14 to 16 hours on, 8 to 10 off.</li>
  <li><strong>Herbs (basil, cilantro, mint):</strong> 12 to 14 hours on.</li>
  <li><strong>Houseplants (pothos, monstera):</strong> 10 to 12 hours on.</li>
  <li><strong>Fruiting plants (tomatoes, peppers, strawberries):</strong> 14 to 16 hours on, but they need the dark to set flowers properly.</li>
</ul>

<p>Everything sits between 10 and 16 hours. Nothing indoor wants 18, and nothing wants 24. If you are unsure, set 14 hours. It is the safe middle that works for most food crops.</p>

<p>Here is why that matters: <strong>running 24/7 does not just waste electricity. It slows growth.</strong> My 4 light strips at 40W each cost about 6 cents an hour to run together. Leaving them on an extra 8 hours a day wasted roughly $1.70 a month and grew worse plants. A timer plug pays for itself in electricity savings alone.</p>

<div class="callout">
  <div class="callout-title">Quick Math</div>
  <p>My 4-shelf light setup draws about 160W total. At 14 hours a day that is 67 kWh a month, about $8. At 24 hours it is 115 kWh, about $14. You pay more and get less.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="darkness">2. Why the Dark Period Matters (My $40 Mistake)</h2>

<p>I thought more light was the whole game. Wattage, then more hours, then more wattage. When my first batch of microgreens came up tall and pale, I assumed they wanted more light. I left the strip on all night.</p>

<p>The next morning they were worse. Leggier. Falling over. I had the number exactly backwards.</p>

<p>Then I remembered the middle school biology I had forgotten. Plants run two processes. In light, photosynthesis makes sugar. In darkness, respiration turns that sugar into actual growth. Pac-Man munching dots by day, then digesting them at night. Take away the night and the plant has nowhere to turn the sugar into new leaves.</p>

<p>Seedlings without a dark period stretch, reach, and fall. That is not a light-intensity problem. That is a broken daily rhythm. Give them a real night and they grow stocky and bounce back in days.</p>

<p>Here is the part most guides skip: <strong>the dark does not have to be pitch black, but it should be dark.</strong> Drifting light from a TV or a hallway lamp is fine. A bright work light aimed at your shelf all night is not.</p>

<h2 id="hours">3. Exact Hours by Plant Type</h2>

<p>Different plants run on different clocks. Here is the map I use, based on 2 years of fiddling:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Lettuce and salad greens:</strong> 14 to 16 hours. They are day-length tolerant and grow fast under long light. My lettuce heads love 15.</li>
  <li><strong>Microgreens before harvest (7 to 14 days):</strong> 12 to 14 hours keeps them stocky and green without bolting.</li>
  <li><strong>Basil:</strong> 14 hours makes it bushy. Under 12 and it gets stringy.</li>
  <li><strong>Spinach and cilantro:</strong> 12 to 13 hours. Here is the hidden door: run these too long and they bolt and turn bitter. 12 is the sweet spot.</li>
  <li><strong>Houseplants:</strong> 10 to 12 hours is plenty. They mostly want to survive, not explode.</li>
</ul>

<p>I linked the <a href="/grow-lights" target="_blank">full grow light database</a> if you want to match a specific light to your plant and wattage first.</p>

<p>The one crop that breaks the rule: fruiting plants. Tomato, pepper, and strawberry need 14 to 16 hours of light, but crucially they also need the dark to trigger flowering. Leave strawberries on 24/7 and you get leaves and no fruit. I learned that the hard way with a berry-less summer.</p>

<h2 id="seedlings">4. Seedlings: More Hours, But Not 24/7</h2>

<p>Seedlings want the upper end, 14 to 16 hours, because they are building roots and structure fast. The famous 16-hour recommendation exists for a reason. My garden seedlings under 16 hours came up twice as stocky as the 12-hour batch.</p>

<p>But here is the part nobody tells beginners: <strong>seedlings also need the night.</strong> You do not get more growth by pushing from 16 to 20 hours. You get the opposite: stretched, weak, falling-over starts. I killed two early trays chasing "more is better."</p>

<p>That is where the light placement matters too. Hours and distance work together. Too close plus too long equals burned or stunted. Too far plus long equals leggy. I keep my strips about 6 inches above seedlings running 15 hours. I wrote the <a href="/posts/grow-light-distance-chart">full grow light distance chart</a> for the spacing half, and this guide covers the timing half.</p>

<p>If your seedlings are still stretching with correct hours, it is almost always distance, not duration. Fix the gap first before you touch the timer.</p>

<h2 id="signs">5. The 3 Signs Your Light Is On Too Long</h2>

<p>How do you know before it is too late? Watch for these:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Leaves curling down at the edges.</strong> Too much light stress. The plant is trying to shrink its surface area.</li>
  <li><strong>Pale or washed-out new growth.</strong> This is over-exposure, not "not enough." Back the hours off.</li>
  <li><strong>Slow growth despite lots of light.</strong> The classic 24/7 trap. The plant has no dark phase, so it is not actually growing larger.</li>
</ol>

<p>Fix all three the same way: drop to 12 hours for a few days and watch. Greens bounce back fast. My stressed microgreens recovered within 72 hours of getting a real night.</p>

<p>Note the catch: <a href="/posts/how-to-fix-leggy-seedlings">leggy seedlings</a> can also mean too little light. The two failure modes look similar. The difference is the leaf: under-lighted starts are pale green and reaching. Over-lighted ones curl and burn. Look at the leaf, not just the stem.</p>

<h2 id="timer">6. Set It and Forget It: The $10 Fix</h2>

<p>You will never remember to unplug a light every night at 9pm. Nobody does. That is why every serious setup starts with a timer plug. A basic mechanical one costs about $10.</p>

<p>Here is what I actually use and why it stopped my forgetfulness cold:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mechanical+outlet+timer+plug&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">mechanical outlet timer</a>.</strong> Around $10. Flip the pins, plug in the light, done. No app, nothing to pair. It just works. This is the one I run for microgreens and herbs.</li>
  <li><strong>A programmable <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=outlet+light+timer+digital&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">digital timer</a></strong> if you want different schedules for different shelves. About $15.</li>
</ul>

<p>Wire the timer between the wall and your power strip, then set the strip to 7am on, 9pm off. That is 14 hours, the sweet spot. You set it once and never think about it again.</p>

<p>One honest thing: mechanical timers tick quietly and can make a faint click when they switch. I do not notice it anymore, but my first one startled me at midnight the first night. If that would drive you crazy, get the digital one. Both work, both cost under $20.</p>

<p>This is the part that matters. <strong>The hours question is not about adding more light. It is about giving the plant a rhythm.</strong> My biggest indoor wins came from one change: turning the light off at night. Everything I grow now runs on a timer and a real dark period, and everything is stockier for it.</p>

<p>Set your light for 14 hours, give the plants a real night, and let the timer do the remembering. It is the single cheapest upgrade to any indoor grow setup. <a href="https://www.amazon.com/s?k=mechanical+outlet+timer+plug&tag=tabletopharve-20" target="_blank" rel="nofollow sponsored">Check current timer prices on Amazon</a>: the one I use was about $10 when I last checked, and it bounces between $8 and $13. If it is under $12, that is a solid deal.</p>

